"**1. Summary**\n\n**a. Verdict**  \nI have proved that for every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;k\\in\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}\\;}\n\\]\n\ni.e. **every** non\u2011negative integer not exceeding \\(n\\) can occur as the number of sunny lines in a configuration of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the covering condition.\n\n**b. Method Sketch**  \n\n* **Step\u202f1 \u2013 Geometry of the required points.**  \n  The points that have to be covered are  \n  \\[\n  T_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n  \\]\n  They lie on the \\(n\\) lines\n  \\[\n  L_s:\\;x+y=s\\qquad (s=2,3,\\dots ,n+1),\n  \\]\n  each of slope \\(-1\\).  Hence the whole set \\(T_n\\) can be covered by the \\(n\\) non\u2011sunny lines \\(\\{L_s\\}_{s=2}^{n+1}\\).\n\n* **Step\u202f2 \u2013 Replacing a non\u2011sunny line by sunny ones.**  \n  Fix a diagonal \\(L_s\\) (so \\(s\\) is fixed).  The points of \\(T_n\\) on \\(L_s\\) are  \n  \\[\n  P_{s}=\\{(a,s-a)\\mid 1\\le a\\le s-1\\}.\n  \\]\n  Choose any integer \\(t\\) with \\(1\\le t\\le |P_s|\\).  \n  Pick \\(t\\) distinct points of \\(P_s\\) and, for each of them, draw a line of **any** slope different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\) (i.e. a sunny line) that passes through the chosen point and through no other point of \\(T_n\\).  \n  The remaining \\(|P_s|-t\\) points of \\(P_s\\) are still covered by the original diagonal \\(L_s\\).  \n  Thus we have replaced one non\u2011sunny line by \\(t\\) sunny lines while keeping the total number of lines equal to \\(n\\).\n\n* **Step\u202f3 \u2013 Achieving any prescribed number of sunny lines.**  \n  Starting from the base configuration \\(\\{L_s\\}_{s=2}^{n+1}\\) (which has \\(k=0\\) sunny lines), we repeatedly apply the replacement described in Step\u202f2.  \n  Each replacement increases the number of sunny lines by an arbitrary positive integer not exceeding the number of points on the chosen diagonal.  \n  Because the total number of points in \\(T_n\\) is \\(\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}\\) and each diagonal contains at least one point, by a suitable sequence of replacements we can obtain **any** integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\).  \n  In particular, to obtain a given \\(k\\) we proceed as follows:\n  * write \\(k\\) as a sum of at most \\(n\\) positive integers,\n  * assign each summand to a distinct diagonal,\n  * on the diagonal assigned the summand \\(t\\) replace the diagonal by \\(t\\) sunny lines as in Step\u202f2,\n  * keep the untouched diagonals unchanged (they remain non\u2011sunny).\n\n  The construction yields exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines, of which precisely \\(k\\) are sunny, and every point of \\(T_n\\) lies on at least one of them.\n\n* **Step\u202f4 \u2013 Impossibility of \\(k>n\\).**  \n  There are only \\(n\\) lines in the whole configuration, so obviously we cannot have more than \\(n\\) sunny lines.  Hence the set \\(\\{0,1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) is maximal.\n\nThus every integer between \\(0\\) and \\(n\\) inclusive can occur, and no other integer can.\n\n---\n\n**2. Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad n\\ge 3 .\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 1  \n\\(T_n\\) is the disjoint union of the \\(n\\) lines  \n\n\\[\nL_s:\\;x+y=s\\qquad (s=2,3,\\dots ,n+1),\n\\]\n\neach of slope \\(-1\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nIf \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) then \\(s:=a+b\\) satisfies \\(2\\le s\\le n+1\\); conversely, for any such \\(s\\) the integer solutions of \\(a+b=s\\) with \\(a,b\\ge 1\\) are exactly the points of \\(T_n\\) lying on \\(L_s\\). \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 2  \nFor any diagonal \\(L_s\\) and any integer \\(t\\) with \\(1\\le t\\le |P_s|\\) (where \\(P_s:=L_s\\cap T_n\\)), there exist \\(t\\) distinct sunny lines each passing through a distinct point of \\(P_s\\) and through no other point of \\(T_n\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nWrite \\(P_s=\\{(a_i,s-a_i)\\mid i=1,\\dots ,|P_s|\\}\\) with the \\(a_i\\) distinct.  \nChoose any \\(t\\) of these points, say \\((a_{i_j},s-a_{i_j})\\) for \\(j=1,\\dots ,t\\).  \nFor each chosen point pick a slope \\(\\lambda_j\\) that is **different** from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\) and also different from the slopes of the lines already constructed.  \nDefine the line  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_j:\\; y-(s-a_{i_j})=\\lambda_j\\bigl(x-a_{i_j}\\bigr).\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(\\lambda_j\\neq -1\\), \\(\\ell_j\\) is not parallel to any line \\(L_{s'}\\); because \\(\\lambda_j\\neq 0,\\infty\\) it is not horizontal or vertical.  Hence each \\(\\ell_j\\) is sunny.  \n\nIf \\(\\ell_j\\) contained another point \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) with \\((a,b)\\neq (a_{i_j},s-a_{i_j})\\), then we would have  \n\n\\[\nb-(s-a_{i_j})=\\lambda_j\\bigl(a-a_{i_j}\\bigr),\n\\qquad a+b\\le n+1 .\n\\]\n\nSince the left\u2011hand side is an integer and \\(\\lambda_j\\) is a rational number (chosen arbitrarily), we can avoid this by choosing \\(\\lambda_j\\) irrational; then the equality cannot hold for integer \\((a,b)\\).  Thus each \\(\\ell_j\\) meets \\(T_n\\) only at its designated point. \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 3  \nFor every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) there exists a family \\(\\mathcal F_k\\) of exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines such that  \n\n* exactly \\(k\\) lines of \\(\\mathcal F_k\\) are sunny,\n* every point of \\(T_n\\) lies on at least one line of \\(\\mathcal F_k\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nStart from the base family  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal B=\\{L_s\\mid s=2,3,\\dots ,n+1\\},\n\\]\n\nwhich contains \\(n\\) non\u2011sunny lines and covers \\(T_n\\) by Lemma\u202f1.\n\nWrite \\(k\\) as a sum of at most \\(n\\) positive integers,\n\\[\nk=t_2+t_3+\\dots +t_{n+1},\n\\]\nwhere each \\(t_s\\) satisfies \\(0\\le t_s\\le |P_s|\\) and all but at most \\(n\\) of the \\(t_s\\) are zero (this is always possible because \\(\\sum_{s=2}^{n+1}|P_s|=|T_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}\\ge n\\)).  \n\nFor each \\(s\\) with \\(t_s>0\\) apply Lemma\u202f2 to the diagonal \\(L_s\\) with the integer \\(t_s\\).  Replace the line \\(L_s\\) in \\(\\mathcal B\\) by the \\(t_s\\) sunny lines obtained.  The lines that replace different diagonals are distinct because they have different slopes (chosen distinct in Lemma\u202f2).  \n\nAfter performing this replacement for every \\(s\\) we obtain a family \\(\\mathcal F_k\\) consisting of  \n\n\\[\n\\underbrace{(n-\\#\\{s\\mid t_s>0\\})}_{\\text{remaining original diagonals}}\n\\;+\\;\\underbrace{\\sum_{s} t_s}_{=k}\n\\;=\\;n\n\\]\n\nlines.  Exactly the \\(k\\) newly introduced lines are sunny; the others are the untouched diagonals, which have slope \\(-1\\) and are therefore non\u2011sunny.  By construction each point of \\(T_n\\) either lies on its original diagonal (if that diagonal was not replaced) or on the sunny line that was created for it, so the covering condition holds. \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 4  \nNo configuration of \\(n\\) distinct lines can contain more than \\(n\\) sunny lines.\n\n*Proof.*  \nThe configuration has exactly \\(n\\) lines; a line is either sunny or not, so the number of sunny lines cannot exceed the total number of lines. \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Theorem  \nFor a given integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is precisely  \n\n\\[\n\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}.\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  \n*Existence.*  Lemma\u202f3 shows that for every \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) there is a configuration of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the covering condition and having exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n*Non\u2011existence for larger \\(k\\).*  Lemma\u202f4 shows that a configuration of \\(n\\) lines cannot have more than \\(n\\) sunny lines.  Hence no \\(k>n\\) is possible.\n\nTherefore the admissible values are exactly the integers from \\(0\\) to \\(n\\). \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Conclusion.**  \nFor every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) the possible numbers of sunny lines in a suitable collection of \\(n\\) distinct lines are precisely  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{0,1,2,\\dots ,n}.\n\\]"
